### Describe the problem that you experienced

The `LOCALE_ID` API documentation shows only `{provide: LOCALE_ID, useValue: 'en-US'}` in both the standalone and module examples. `useValue` works for a static string or a value computed before the injector exists (e.g., reading `navigator.language` at module load).

If the locale logic already lives in an Angular service (user preferences, route params, cookies, etc.), `useValue` can't reach it because there's no injection context at that point. The clean path is `useFactory` with `inject()`, and the current docs don't show that's supported. A developer landing on the API page after searching "Angular locale" doesn't realize the service case is a one-liner.

A suggested third example:

```ts
@Injectable({providedIn: 'root'})
export class LangService {
  getLocale(): string {
    // read from user prefs, cookie, navigator, etc.
    return 'en-US';
  }
}

const appConfig: ApplicationConfig = {
  providers: [
    {
      provide: LOCALE_ID,
      useFactory: () => inject(LangService).getLocale(),
    },
  ],
};
```

Happy to send a PR if the team agrees this is a worthwhile addition.

### Describe the problem that you experienced


The "Forwarding injected dependencies" section in `adev/src/content/guide/components/inheritance.md` (around line 66) teaches the constructor-injection forwarding pattern:

```ts
export class ListboxBase {
  constructor(private element: ElementRef) {}
}

export class CustomListbox extends ListboxBase {
  constructor(element: ElementRef) {
    super(element);
  }
}
```

But the style guide says to use `inject()` instead of constructor parameter injection. And once you do, this whole "forwarding" thing kind of evaporates, the child just inherits the property:

```ts
export class ListboxBase {
  protected element = inject(ElementRef);
}

export class CustomListbox extends ListboxBase {
  // element is already there. no constructor needed.
}
```

The example earlier in this same file (line 38ish) already uses signal-based `input.required<string>()`, so this section sticks out as the only place still showing the old pattern.

Happy to put up a PR if this sounds reasonable.


