## Summary

When SSE or WebSocket streams feed data into a Svelte store, updating the store on every incoming chunk causes UI hangs — sometimes complete freeze in embedded webviews (Tauri, Electron, Wails). This is a common, non-obvious pitfall for anyone building AI chat UIs, real-time dashboards, or streaming data apps with Svelte.

The fix is a short `requestAnimationFrame` batching pattern, but it is not documented anywhere in the Svelte docs or cookbook.

## The Problem

A naive SSE consumer:
\`\`\`typescript
source.onmessage = (e) => {
    myStore.update(s => s + e.data); // fires 50-100x per response
};
\`\`\`

Each update triggers `$:` reactive blocks, `afterUpdate` callbacks, and any subscribers. At 50-100 chunks/response, this floods the microtask queue and blocks the main thread.

## The Fix — requestAnimationFrame Batching

\`\`\`typescript
let buffer = '';
let rafHandle: number | null = null;

source.onmessage = (e) => {
    buffer += e.data;
    if (!rafHandle) {
        rafHandle = requestAnimationFrame(() => {
            myStore.update(s => s + buffer); // one store write per frame
            buffer = '';
            rafHandle = null;
        });
    }
};
\`\`\`

**Effect:** Reduces store update frequency from 50-100x to 3-5x per response (one per animation frame). UI stays responsive at any stream speed.

## Alternative: component-local buffer

For maximum control, keep the buffer in a local variable and only push to the store on completion:
\`\`\`typescrip... [TRUNCATED]

