### Describe the problem

I use enum very frequently in my project, so I hope there's a `{#match}` in the template instead of using multiple `{#if}` and `{:else if}`.

Assume we have a enum:

```ts
enum Foo { A, B, C }
```

The template would be like this:

```svelte
{#if x == Foo.A}
    Lorem Ipsum
{:else if x == Foo.B}
    Quick Brown Fox
{:else if x == Foo.C}
    I can eat glass.
{:else}
    The Default Case
{/if}
```

As you can see, `x ==` is repeated in every branch, and the number of characters in `{#if` and `{:else if` are different, which looks ugly as they're not aligned.

### Describe the proposed solution

```svelte
{#match x}
    The Default Case
{:case Foo.A}
    Lorem Ipsum
{:case Foo.B}
    Quick Brown Fox
{:case Foo.C}
    I can eat glass.
{/match}
```

We can type less if we had this syntax, and every case is beautifully aligned.
The LSP could also suggest an AST transformation that adds all the missing cases for an enum.
If possible, we can even pattern match on tuples.

But there must be someone hates putting the default case to the top. That's because it prevents the markup from being indented twice as in Python and other common style for C-like languages. So we could have syntax like this if double indent is acceptable:

```svelte
{#match x}
    {:case Foo.A}
        Lorem Ipsum
    {:case Foo.B}
        Quick Brown Fox
    {:case Foo.C}
        I can eat glass.
    {:default}
        The Default Case
    {/default}
{/match}
```

