let seedDateTemporal = $state(Temporal.PlainDate.from("2026-01-01"));
const weekStartTemporal = $derived(
    seedDateTemporal.subtract({ days: seedDateTemporal.dayOfWeek }),
);
```

If `seedDateTemporal` is updated to Jan 2nd, any dependencies of `weekStartTemporal` will rerun even though the value hasn't changed, just the objects don't referentially match.

### Describe the proposed solution

It would be nice if, in addition to `===`, Svelte also check if the new derived object has an `.equals()` method.  If it does, pass the old value as the first parameter and if the response equals `true` then don't mark the children of the derived as dirty.

Something like this:
```javascript
function safeEqualsCheck(obj1, obj2) {
    try {
        if (obj1 === obj2 || obj1.equals(obj2) === true) {
            return true
        }
    } finally {
        return false
    }
}
```

### Importance

would make my life easier

### Describe the bug

The CSS hash is, by default, computed based on the contents of `<style>`. But this isn't the only thing that determines the output CSS — if an element goes from unused to uses, the output CSS will reflect that but the hash won't.

In esoteric setups, this can mean that updated styles aren't applied to new elements

