### Describe the problem

When composing Svelte components that each define their own scoped styles, only the innermost child component's hash is applied to the rendered element. This prevents parent component styles from working correctly, breaking component composition patterns.

## Reproduction
Here's a minimal reproduction showing a `Heading` component that wraps a `Text` component:
### Text.svelte
```svelte
<script>
  let { tag = "div", class: className, children, ...props } = $props();
</script>

<svelte:element this={tag} class={["text", className]} {...props}>
  {@render children?.()}
</svelte:element>

<style>
  .text {
    color: gray;
    line-height: 1.5;
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 1rem;
  }
</style>
```
### Heading.svelte
```svelte
<script>
  import Text from './Text.svelte';
  let { tag = "h2", class: className, children, ...props } = $props();
</script>

<Text {tag} class={["heading", className]} {...props}>
  {@render children?.()}
</Text>

<style>
  .heading {
    color: black;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
</style>
```
### Expected Output
```svelte
<h2 class="text heading s-TextHash s-HeadingHash">Content</h2>
```
### Output
```svelte
<h2 class="text heading s-TextHash">Content</h2>
```
Only the `Text` component's hash is applied. The `.heading` styles defined in `Heading.svelte` don't work because the `s-HeadingHash` is missing.
