Hello,

Using the built-in `http` and `https` packages causes a memory leak when the server gets flooded with requests from different IPs which eventually crashes the server.

**Code**
```js
const express = require('express');
const fs = require('fs');
const http = require('http');
const https = require('https');

const app = express();

app.enable('trust proxy');

app.all('*', (req, res) => {
  return res.send({
    message: 'Hello World!'
  });
});

const privateKey = fs.readFileSync('/etc/letsencrypt/live/.../privkey.pem', 'utf8');
const certificate = fs.readFileSync('/etc/letsencrypt/live/.../cert.pem', 'utf8');
const ca = fs.readFileSync('/etc/letsencrypt/live/.../chain.pem', 'utf8');
const credentials = {
    key: privateKey,
    cert: certificate,
    ca: ca
};

const httpServer = http.createServer(app);
const httpsServer = https.createServer(credentials, app);

httpServer.listen(80, () => {
    console.log('HTTP Server running on port 80');
});

httpsServer.listen(443, () => {
    console.log('HTTPS Server running on port 443');
});
```

**Error**
```
(node:14043) MaxListenersExceededWarning: Possible EventEmitter memory leak detected. 11 error listeners added to [TLSSocket]. Use emitter.setMaxListeners() to increase limit
```

```js
var express = require('express');

var app = express()

app.use(express.text({ type: '*' }));

app.put('*', (req, res) => {
  console.log('Request Body:', req.body);
  res.end();
});
```
now If you send a `PUT` request without `Content-Type` header, it will print this:
```js
Request Body: undefined
```
Which means `express.text` didn't work and thus `req.body` remained unpopulated.

**Workarounds**
Although a workaround like this works but it doesn't feel right. `type: '*'` is supposed to be able to handle everything.
```js
// working workaround
express.text({ type: () => true });
```

**Working Implementations**
https://github.com/touhidurrr/UncivServer.xyz/blob/03cf8bff12ab5e69fa416291906fae608e04e5bc/src/server.js#L49

Like all `app.METHOD()`, `app.all()` takes callback functions that, according to http://expressjs.com/en/4x/api.html#app.all can be

- A middleware function.
- A series of middleware functions (separated by commas).
- An array of middleware functions.
